  "schema:hasPart": [
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0001-p0",
      "beacon:sectionType": "introduction",
      "schema:text": "This introduction section from the article discusses the broad definition of cancer, its historical etymology, and the classification of various malignancies, establishing the context for exploring new therapeutic approaches like ferroptosis.\n\nCancer encompasses a diverse group of diseases characterized by uncontrolled cell growth and proliferation. The term \"cancer,\" derived from the Greek word \"karkinos\" coined by Hippocrates, the \"Father of Medicine,\" has evolved to describe over 277 distinct types of malignancies [ 5 , 31 ]. Historically, the term was translated into Latin by the Roman surgeon Celsus, linking it to the brachyuran crab, a metaphor for the disease's invasive nature. Globally, cancer is the second leading cause of mortality, with 18.1 million new cases reported in 2018, the highest incidence occurring in Asia ( Fig. 1",
      "schema:name": "Introduction",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "cancer",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "METHOD"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Hippocrates",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Celsus",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "karkinos",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "brachyuran crab",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "18.1 million",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "2018",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "cancer",
          "beacon:predicate": "relates_to",
          "beacon:object": "karkinos",
          "beacon:evidence": "the term 'cancer,' derived from the Greek word 'karkinos' coined by Hippocrates"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "cancer",
          "beacon:predicate": "relates_to",
          "beacon:object": "Celsus",
          "beacon:evidence": "the term was translated into Latin by the Roman surgeon Celsus"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "Celsus",
          "beacon:predicate": "relates_to",
          "beacon:object": "brachyuran crab",
          "beacon:evidence": "linking it to the brachyuran crab, a metaphor for the disease's invasive nature"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "cancer",
          "beacon:predicate": "produces",
          "beacon:object": "18.1 million new cases",
          "beacon:evidence": "with 18.1 million new cases reported in 2018"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0001-p1",
      "beacon:sectionType": "introduction",
      "schema:text": "This introduction section from the article discusses the multifaceted nature of cancer as a disease, focusing on the pathological hallmark of neoplasia and its implications for tumor invasion and patient outcomes.\n\n1 Cancer can be conceptualized as a multifaceted illness, characterized by complex tempo-spatial alterations in cell physiology that culminate in the formation of malignant tumors [ 18 ]. The pathological hallmark of cancer is neoplasia, or abnormal cell proliferation, which drives tumor invasion into surrounding tissues and distant organs, contributing significantly to patient morbidity and mortality. The transformation of normal cells into malignant cancer cells has been a focal point of biomedical research for decades [ 76 ].",
      "schema:name": "Introduction",
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "cancer",
          "beacon:predicate": "relates_to",
          "beacon:object": "neoplasia",
          "beacon:evidence": "The pathological hallmark of cancer is neoplasia, or abnormal cell proliferation."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "neoplasia",
          "beacon:predicate": "causes",
          "beacon:object": "tumor invasion",
          "beacon:evidence": "neoplasia... drives tumor invasion into surrounding tissues and distant organs."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "tumor invasion",
          "beacon:predicate": "contributes",
          "beacon:object": "patient morbidity and mortality",
          "beacon:evidence": "tumor invasion... contributing significantly to patient morbidity and mortality."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0001-p2",
      "beacon:sectionType": "introduction",
      "schema:text": "This introduction section from the article discusses the ongoing research on the transformation of normal cells into cancer cells and highlights recent advances in identifying therapeutic targets related to approximately 300 genes involved in human cancer.\n\nThe transformation of normal cells into malignant cancer cells has been a focal point of biomedical research for decades [ 76 ]. In recent years, significant advances have been made in identifying therapeutic targets by elucidating the distinctions between cancer cells and their healthy counterparts. This research has led to the identification of approximately 300 genes implicated in human cancer, as cataloged in the Cancer Gene Census (Futreal et al., 2004; [ 9 ]). These genes predominantly play critical roles in signal transduction, cell cycle regulation, apoptosis, angiogenesis, and cellular infiltration (Vogelstein and Kinzler, 2004). Targeting ferroptosis in cancer stems from its unique iron-dependent, lipid peroxidation-driven cell death mechanism, offering an alternative pathway to overcome resistance to apoptosis, a common hallmark of cancer. Many tumors exhibit dysregulated iron metabolism or altered lipid composition, rendering them potentially susceptible to ferroptosis induction [ 44 ].",
      "schema:name": "Introduction",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Cancer Gene Census",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Futreal et al.",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "Cancer Gene Census",
          "beacon:predicate": "associated_with",
          "beacon:object": "approximately 300 genes",
          "beacon:evidence": "cataloged in the Cancer Gene Census"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0001-p3",
      "beacon:sectionType": "introduction",
      "schema:text": "This introduction section from the article discusses the potential of ferroptosis in cancer therapy by highlighting its effects on tumor iron metabolism, lipid composition, and its synergy with immunotherapy to enhance anti-tumor immune responses.\n\nMany tumors exhibit dysregulated iron metabolism or altered lipid composition, rendering them potentially susceptible to ferroptosis induction [ 44 ]. Furthermore, the ability of ferroptosis to elicit immunogenic cell death (ICD) presents an opportunity to synergize with immunotherapy, enhancing anti-tumor immune responses. This distinct mechanism, therefore, offers a promising avenue for novel cancer therapies. In addition, understanding the genetic and molecular underpinnings of cancer is essential for developing targeted therapies and improving patient outcomes. Ongoing research continues to uncover the complexities of cancer biology, aiming to translate these findings into effective clinical interventions.",
      "schema:name": "Introduction",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "immunogenic cell death",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "immunotherapy",
          "beacon:entityType": "TECHNOLOGY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "cancer therapy",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:predicate": "supports",
          "beacon:object": "immunotherapy",
          "beacon:evidence": "synergy with immunotherapy to enhance anti-tumor immune responses."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:predicate": "causes",
          "beacon:object": "immunogenic cell death",
          "beacon:evidence": "ability of ferroptosis to elicit immunogenic cell death (ICD)"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:predicate": "relates_to",
          "beacon:object": "cancer therapy",
          "beacon:evidence": "potential of ferroptosis in cancer therapy"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0001-p4",
      "beacon:sectionType": "introduction",
      "schema:text": "This introduction section from the article discusses the nature of tumors, detailing their formation from rapid cell division and their variation in size and location within the body.\n\nOngoing research continues to uncover the complexities of cancer biology, aiming to translate these findings into effective clinical interventions. ### Tumors\n\nA tumor arises from the rapid division of cells, resulting in abnormal tissue masses or lumps [ 17 ]. Tumors can vary significantly in size, from small nodules to large masses, and can develop in virtually any part of the body.",
      "schema:name": "Introduction",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Tumors",
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "tumor",
          "beacon:predicate": "arises_from",
          "beacon:object": "rapid division of cells",
          "beacon:evidence": "A tumor arises from the rapid division of cells"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0001-p5",
      "beacon:sectionType": "introduction",
      "schema:text": "This introduction section from a CTARC article discusses the variability of tumors in size and location, and outlines the classification of tumors into benign types.\n\nTumors can vary significantly in size, from small nodules to large masses, and can develop in virtually any part of the body. Types of Tumors\n\nTumors are classified into three main categories: 1. Benign Tumors : These tumors are non-cancerous and do not exhibit malignant behavior. They typically grow slowly, if at all, and rarely recur after surgical removal. Common examples include hemangiomas and fibroids [ 10 ]. 2. Premalignant Tumors : These lesions, such as cervical dysplasia and actinic keratosis, contain cells that have not yet become cancerous but possess the potential to progress to malignancy [ 10 ]. 3. Malignant Tumors : Malignant tumors are cancerous and characterized by uncontrolled cell proliferation and the ability to metastasize to different body regions [ 10 ]. Examples include various forms of cancer, germ cell tumors, and blastomas.",
      "schema:name": "Introduction",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Tumors",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "hemangiomas",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "fibroids",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "cervical dysplasia",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "actinic keratosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "hemangiomas",
          "beacon:predicate": "associated_with",
          "beacon:object": "benign tumors",
          "beacon:evidence": "Common examples include hemangiomas and fibroids."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "fibroids",
          "beacon:predicate": "associated_with",
          "beacon:object": "benign tumors",
          "beacon:evidence": "Common examples include hemangiomas and fibroids."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "cervical dysplasia",
          "beacon:predicate": "associated_with",
          "beacon:object": "premalignant tumors",
          "beacon:evidence": "such as cervical dysplasia and actinic keratosis."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "actinic keratosis",
          "beacon:predicate": "associated_with",
          "beacon:object": "premalignant tumors",
          "beacon:evidence": "such as cervical dysplasia and actinic keratosis."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0001-p6",
      "beacon:sectionType": "introduction",
      "schema:text": "This introduction section from the article discusses various types of cancer, highlighting examples such as breast cancer and prostate cancer, and their distinct characteristics relevant to treatment approaches.\n\nExamples include various forms of cancer, germ cell tumors, and blastomas. ### Types of Cancer\n\nCancer manifests in various forms, each with distinct characteristics and implications for treatment: • Breast Cancer : This is one of the most prevalent cancers globally, primarily affecting women, though it can also occur in men [ 4 ]. • Prostate Cancer : Originating in the prostate gland, this cancer is common among men and varies in aggressiveness [ 71 ]. • Basal Cell Carcinoma : The most common form of skin cancer, it arises from basal cells in the epidermis. Early detection typically leads to successful treatment [ 62 ]. • Melanoma : This cancer originates in melanocytes, the pigment-producing cells of the skin [ 69 ]. • Colon Cancer : Also known as colorectal cancer, it begins in the large intestine and often starts as a polyp that may develop into cancer over time [ 61 ]. • Lung Cancer : Characterized by uncontrolled growth of abnormal lung cells, lung cancer can metastasize and is categorized into small cell lung cancer (SCLC) and non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) [ 84 ]. • Leukemia : This cancer involves the proliferation of abnormal white blood cells, impairing the body's ability to produce healthy blood cells [ 60 ]. • Lymphoma : Affecting the lymphatic system, lymphoma originates in lymphocytes and can spread to other organs. It is classified into Hodgkin lymphoma (HL) and non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) [ 38 ].",
      "schema:name": "Introduction",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Types of Cancer",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "breast cancer",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "prostate cancer",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "germ cell tumors",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "blastomas",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Basal Cell Carcinoma",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "breast cancer",
          "beacon:predicate": "associated_with",
          "beacon:object": "distinct characteristics",
          "beacon:evidence": "breast cancer and prostate cancer, and their distinct characteristics relevant to treatment approaches."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "prostate cancer",
          "beacon:predicate": "associated_with",
          "beacon:object": "distinct characteristics",
          "beacon:evidence": "breast cancer and prostate cancer, and their distinct characteristics relevant to treatment approaches."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "Basal Cell Carcinoma",
          "beacon:predicate": "produces",
          "beacon:object": "skin cancer",
          "beacon:evidence": "The most common form of skin cancer, it arises from basal cells in the epidermis."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0001-p7",
      "beacon:sectionType": "introduction",
      "schema:text": "This introduction section from the article discusses the primary causes of cancer, highlighting DNA mutations and the various factors that contribute to these changes, including age, environmental influences, and lifestyle choices.\n\nIt is classified into Hodgkin lymphoma (HL) and non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) [ 38 ]. ### Causes of Cancer\n\nThe primary cause of cancer is mutations in a cell's DNA, which can disrupt normal cellular functions and lead to uncontrolled growth [ 65 ]. These mutations can occur due to various factors, including age, environmental influences, and lifestyle choices.",
      "schema:name": "Introduction",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Causes of Cancer",
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "cancer",
          "beacon:predicate": "causes",
          "beacon:object": "mutations in a cell's DNA",
          "beacon:evidence": "The primary cause of cancer is mutations in a cell's DNA."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "mutations in a cell's DNA",
          "beacon:predicate": "produces",
          "beacon:object": "uncontrolled growth",
          "beacon:evidence": "which can disrupt normal cellular functions and lead to uncontrolled growth."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "mutations in a cell's DNA",
          "beacon:predicate": "requires",
          "beacon:object": "age",
