    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0001-p8",
      "beacon:sectionType": "introduction",
      "schema:text": "This introduction section from the article discusses the causes of cancer, highlighting the role of mutations influenced by age, environmental factors, and biological predispositions.\n\nThese mutations can occur due to various factors, including age, environmental influences, and lifestyle choices. Biological Factors\n\nCertain biological factors, such as age, sex, and inherited genetic abnormalities, can predispose individuals to cancer. For instance, blood cancers are more prevalent in older adults due to genetic exchanges between chromosomes [ 33 ]. The Philadelphia chromosome (Ph1), a biomarker in chronic myeloid leukemia, exemplifies how specific genetic mutations can aid in diagnosis [ 22 ].",
      "schema:name": "Introduction",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Causes of Cancer",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Philadelphia chromosome",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "chronic myeloid leukemia",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "Philadelphia chromosome",
          "beacon:predicate": "associated_with",
          "beacon:object": "chronic myeloid leukemia",
          "beacon:evidence": "The Philadelphia chromosome (Ph1), a biomarker in chronic myeloid leukemia"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0001-p9",
      "beacon:sectionType": "introduction",
      "schema:text": "This introduction section from the article discusses the role of specific genetic mutations, such as the Philadelphia chromosome, and environmental factors in the incidence and development of cancer.\n\nThe Philadelphia chromosome (Ph1), a biomarker in chronic myeloid leukemia, exemplifies how specific genetic mutations can aid in diagnosis [ 22 ]. Environmental Factors\n\nCancer incidence varies geographically, suggesting that environmental exposures play a significant role in cancer development. Immigrants often develop cancer types prevalent in their new countries, indicating that environmental rather than inherited factors are crucial [ 32 , 67 ]. Notable environmental carcinogens include UV radiation and air pollutants [ 26 ].",
      "schema:name": "Introduction",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Causes of Cancer",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Philadelphia chromosome",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "chronic myeloid leukemia",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "UV radiation",
          "beacon:entityType": "TECHNOLOGY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "air pollutants",
          "beacon:entityType": "TECHNOLOGY"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "Philadelphia chromosome",
          "beacon:predicate": "relates_to",
          "beacon:object": "chronic myeloid leukemia",
          "beacon:evidence": "The Philadelphia chromosome (Ph1), a biomarker in chronic myeloid leukemia, exemplifies how specific genetic mutations"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0001-p10",
      "beacon:sectionType": "introduction",
      "schema:text": "This introduction section from the article discusses notable environmental carcinogens and the impact of occupational hazards on cellular damage and genetic mutations, which significantly contribute to the development of various cancers.\n\nNotable environmental carcinogens include UV radiation and air pollutants [ 26 ]. Occupational and Risk Factors\n\nExposure to hazardous materials and chemicals in the workplace can lead to direct or indirect cellular damage, resulting in genetic mutations [ 6 ]. Such occupational hazards significantly contribute to the development of various cancers.",
      "schema:name": "Introduction",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Causes of Cancer",
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "occupational hazards",
          "beacon:predicate": "causes",
          "beacon:object": "cellular damage",
          "beacon:evidence": "Exposure to hazardous materials and chemicals in the workplace can lead to direct or indirect cellular damage."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "cellular damage",
          "beacon:predicate": "produces",
          "beacon:object": "genetic mutations",
          "beacon:evidence": "resulting in genetic mutations."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "occupational hazards",
          "beacon:predicate": "supports",
          "beacon:object": "development of various cancers",
          "beacon:evidence": "Such occupational hazards significantly contribute to the development of various cancers."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0001-p11",
      "beacon:sectionType": "introduction",
      "schema:text": "This introduction section from the article discusses the causes of cancer, highlighting the impact of occupational hazards and lifestyle-related factors, including smoking, diet, physical activity, and alcohol consumption on cancer risk.\n\nSuch occupational hazards significantly contribute to the development of various cancers. Lifestyle-Related Factors\n\nLifestyle choices significantly impact cancer risk. For example, smoking introduces numerous carcinogenic compounds that are strongly linked to lung cancer [ 2 ]. Other lifestyle factors, such as diet, physical activity, and alcohol consumption, also influence cancer risk.",
      "schema:name": "Introduction",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Causes of Cancer",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "lung cancer",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "smoking",
          "beacon:entityType": "LIFESTYLE"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "diet",
          "beacon:entityType": "LIFESTYLE"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "physical activity",
          "beacon:entityType": "LIFESTYLE"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "alcohol consumption",
          "beacon:entityType": "LIFESTYLE"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "smoking",
          "beacon:predicate": "causes",
          "beacon:object": "lung cancer",
          "beacon:evidence": "smoking introduces numerous carcinogenic compounds that are strongly linked to lung cancer"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0005-p0",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This hallmark of cancer section from the article discusses the regulatory processes governing normal cell behavior and contrasts them with the unregulated growth observed in cancer cells.\n\nThe division, growth, stopping of development, and death of normal cells are all regulated by processes. These normal cells don't divide unless instructed to do so by neighboring cells [ 66 ]. Every tissue maintains its appropriate size and shape in compliance with the body's needs thanks to this ongoing cooperation [ 3 ]. On the other hand, cancer cells oppose this strategy; they ignore the typical growth-control signals and instead follow their own internal reproductive agenda [ 18 ].",
      "schema:name": "Hallmark of Cancer"
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0005-p1",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This Hallmark of Cancer section from Oluwafemi Adeleke Ojo's article discusses how cancer cells defy growth-control signals and invade surrounding tissues, highlighting their ability to spread and form metastases.\n\nOn the other hand, cancer cells oppose this strategy; they ignore the typical growth-control signals and instead follow their own internal reproductive agenda [ 18 ]. They also possess a far more cunning characteristic: they could disperse from their original location, intruding on nearby tissues and building up into masses in other areas of the body. The deviation from this natural defense mechanisms which is very common in most Cancers leads to the development of Tumor.",
      "schema:name": "Hallmark of Cancer",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Oluwafemi Adeleke Ojo",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Hallmark of Cancer",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Tumor",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "Hallmark of Cancer",
          "beacon:predicate": "supports",
          "beacon:object": "Tumor",
          "beacon:evidence": "the deviation from this natural defense mechanisms... leads to the development of Tumor."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0005-p2",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This hallmark of cancer section from Oluwafemi Adeleke Ojo discusses the mechanisms of applying targeted therapy, focusing on immune evasion and how tumor mutations facilitate the escape from immune detection.\n\nThe deviation from this natural defense mechanisms which is very common in most Cancers leads to the development of Tumor. ### Mechanisms of Applying Targeted Therapy\n\nImmune evasion: Both positive and negative effects on the immune system can result from tumors. Tumor cells rapidly undergo mutations that allow them to proliferate without these antigens and evade detection, but antibodies are formed when the immune system initially identifies tumor antigens [ 27 ].",
      "schema:name": "Hallmark of Cancer",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Mechanisms of Applying Targeted Therapy",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Oluwafemi Adeleke Ojo",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Tumor",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Targeted Therapy",
          "beacon:entityType": "METHOD"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Immune Evasion",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Tumor Mutations",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Antibodies",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Immune Detection",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Tumor Antigens",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "Tumor Mutations",
          "beacon:predicate": "enables",
          "beacon:object": "Immune Evasion",
          "beacon:evidence": "tumor mutations facilitate the escape from immune detection."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "Tumor",
          "beacon:predicate": "causes",
          "beacon:object": "Tumor Mutations",
          "beacon:evidence": "deviation from this natural defense mechanisms...leads to the development of Tumor."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "Tumor Antigens",
          "beacon:predicate": "produces",
          "beacon:object": "Antibodies",
          "beacon:evidence": "antibodies are formed when the immune system initially identifies tumor antigens."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0005-p3",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from CTARC discusses how tumor cells evade immune detection through mutations and the stress responses of hypoxia and DNA damage that ultimately stabilize and support tumor growth.\n\nTumor cells rapidly undergo mutations that allow them to proliferate without these antigens and evade detection, but antibodies are formed when the immune system initially identifies tumor antigens [ 27 ]. Stress response: Hypoxia, or low oxygen and nutrition, stresses tumor cells and increases DNA damage. The body's defensive reaction to stress stabilizes the tumor cell and encourages the tumor's growth [ 41 ].",
      "schema:name": "Hallmark of Cancer",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Mechanisms of Applying Targeted Therapy",
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "tumor cells",
          "beacon:predicate": "evade",
          "beacon:object": "immune detection",
          "beacon:evidence": "tumor cells evade immune detection through mutations"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "hypoxia",
          "beacon:predicate": "supports",
          "beacon:object": "tumor growth",
          "beacon:evidence": "hypoxia ... stabilizes the tumor cell and encourages the tumor's growth"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "DNA damage",
          "beacon:predicate": "supports",
          "beacon:object": "tumor growth",
          "beacon:evidence": "DNA damage ... stabilizes the tumor cell and encourages the tumor's growth"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0005-p4",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the article discusses how the tumor stroma supports cancer growth through cell signaling and mutations, highlighting the mechanisms by which targeted therapies can interact with the tumor microenvironment.\n\nThe body's defensive reaction to stress stabilizes the tumor cell and encourages the tumor's growth [ 41 ]. Stromal subversion : Entire bodily tissue is supported and connected by the stroma, an organ that serves both protective and connective functions. The extracellular matrix, which is made up of stromal cells, contains all other cells. Tumor cell signals induce stromal cell mutations via promoting angiogenesis and tumor cell motility, which permit invasion and metastasis [ 45 ].",
      "schema:name": "Hallmark of Cancer",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Mechanisms of Applying Targeted Therapy",
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "tumor stroma",
          "beacon:predicate": "supports",
          "beacon:object": "cancer growth",
          "beacon:evidence": "the tumor stroma supports cancer growth through cell signaling and mutations"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "tumor cell signals",
          "beacon:predicate": "induce",
          "beacon:object": "stromal cell mutations",
          "beacon:evidence": "Tumor cell signals induce stromal cell mutations via promoting angiogenesis and tumor cell motility"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "stromal cell mutations",
          "beacon:predicate": "permit",
          "beacon:object": "invasion and metastasis",
          "beacon:evidence": "stromal cell mutations via promoting angiogenesis and tumor cell motility, which permit invasion and metastasis"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0005-p5",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This hallmark of cancer section from the article discusses the mechanisms by which tumor cell signals promote stromal cell mutations, angiogenesis, and tumor cell motility, leading to invasion and metastasis through immune cell cytokine release.\n\nTumor cell signals induce stromal cell mutations via promoting angiogenesis and tumor cell motility, which permit invasion and metastasis [ 45 ]. Cytokine factors: In the context of a tumor, immune cells release cytokines, which are proteins that indicate inflammation. As a result of the inflammation that follows, immune cells release substances that encourage angiogenesis, which in turn promotes tumor growth [ 63 ].",
      "schema:name": "Hallmark of Cancer",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Mechanisms of Applying Targeted Therapy",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "tumor cell",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "stromal cell",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "angiogenesis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "tumor cell motility",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "invasion",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "metastasis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "cytokines",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "immune cells",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "tumor cell signals",
          "beacon:predicate": "causes",
          "beacon:object": "stromal cell mutations",
          "beacon:evidence": "tumor cell signals promote stromal cell mutations"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "tumor cell signals",
          "beacon:predicate": "supports",
          "beacon:object": "angiogenesis",
          "beacon:evidence": "tumor cell signals promote stromal cell mutations, angiogenesis"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "tumor cell signals",
          "beacon:predicate": "supports",
          "beacon:object": "tumor cell motility",
          "beacon:evidence": "tumor cell signals promote stromal cell mutations, angiogenesis, and tumor cell motility"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "angiogenesis",
          "beacon:predicate": "enables",
          "beacon:object": "invasion",
          "beacon:evidence": "which permit invasion and metastasis"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "immune cells",
          "beacon:predicate": "produces",
          "beacon:object": "cytokines",
          "beacon:evidence": "immune cells release cytokines, which are proteins that indicate inflammation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0005-p6",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the article discusses unprogrammed cell death, highlighting its characteristics and implications in the context of cancer, particularly in relation to inflammation and tumor growth.\n\nAs a result of the inflammation that follows, immune cells release substances that encourage angiogenesis, which in turn promotes tumor growth [ 63 ]. ### Unprogrammed Cell Death\n\nUnprogrammed cell death, also called accidental cell death or necrosis, happens when cells die without following the controlled procedures of programmed cell death, such as autophagy or apoptosis, instead of dying because of external stimuli or significant damage [ 35 ]. Among them are ferroptosis, necroptotic, and necrosis ( Table 1",
      "schema:name": "Hallmark of Cancer",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Unprogrammed Cell Death",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "unprogrammed cell death",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "cancer",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "inflammation",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "tumor growth",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "angiogenesis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "METHOD"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "necroptotic",
          "beacon:entityType": "METHOD"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "necrosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "METHOD"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "inflammation",
          "beacon:predicate": "causes",
          "beacon:object": "angiogenesis",
          "beacon:evidence": "immune cells release substances that encourage angiogenesis, which in turn promotes tumor growth"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0005-p7",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This hallmarks of cancer section from the article discusses unprogrammed cell death mechanisms, specifically focusing on necrosis and its identification through microscopy techniques.\n\nAmong them are ferroptosis, necroptotic, and necrosis ( Table 1 Necrosis : Necrosis happens when a cell has already experienced significant damage from outside causes like trauma or infection [ 43 ]. Light or electron microscopy can be used to identify cell and organelle enlargement or rupture of surface membranes with leakage of intracellular contents, which contributes to the characterization of necrosis (derived from the Greek word \"nekros,\" which means \"death\") [ 86 ]. The Greek term \"oncosis\" (which means \"swelling\") is sometimes used by researchers, however \"oncotic necrosis\" has also been mentioned. When organellar membranes are damaged, enzymes that degrade proteins can break out from lysosomes, enter the cytoplasm, and eventually kill cells [ 18 ]. Rapid ATP depletion coupled with metabolic failure results in necrosis, which is most frequently observed in ischemia [ 35 ]. Necrosis is characterized by cell swelling along with an uncontrollably large cell membrane rupture that causes the contents of the cell to be expelled [ 43 ].",
      "schema:name": "Hallmark of Cancer",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Unprogrammed Cell Death",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "necrosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "necroptotic",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Light microscopy",
          "beacon:entityType": "TECHNOLOGY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "electron microscopy",
          "beacon:entityType": "TECHNOLOGY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "oncosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "oncotic necrosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "necrosis",
          "beacon:predicate": "associated_with",
          "beacon:object": "Light microscopy",
          "beacon:evidence": "Light or electron microscopy can be used to identify cell and organelle enlargement or rupture of surface membranes"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "necrosis",
          "beacon:predicate": "associated_with",
          "beacon:object": "electron microscopy",
          "beacon:evidence": "Light or electron microscopy can be used to identify cell and organelle enlargement or rupture of surface membranes"
        },
        {
