          "beacon:evidence": "the Greek term 'oncosis' (which means 'swelling') is sometimes used by researchers, however 'oncotic necrosis' has also been mentioned."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0005-p8",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This section from the Hallmark of Cancer discusses unprogrammed cell death, focusing on the characteristics of necrosis and necroptosis as forms of inflammatory cell death and their historical association with pathogen invasion and cellular damage.\n\nNecrosis is characterized by cell swelling along with an uncontrollably large cell membrane rupture that causes the contents of the cell to be expelled [ 43 ]. Necroptosis : It is a mediated type of inflammatory cell death (Shan et al., 2018). Necrosis is historically associated with unprogrammed cell death brought on by pathogen invasion or damage to the cell itself, as opposed to planned programmed cell death brought on by apoptosis [ 43 ]. The discovery of necroptosis demonstrated that necrosis is programmable in cells and that there are other ways for cells to die besides apoptosis [ 30 ]. Moreover, necroptosis' immunogenic properties make it a good option for a number of purposes, such as boosting the immune system's response to pathogens. Necroptosis is a documented defense mechanism that causes the cell to commit \"cellular suicide\" in a caspase-independent way when viral caspase inhibitors are present (Berghe et al., 2014). Necroptosis is only induced by TNF therapy in the presence of a pan-caspase inhibitor, such as VAD fluoroethyl ketone (Linkermann and Green, 2014). It takes a blocked or compromised Caspase for necroptosis to happen. Exposure to each death pathway is regulated (sometimes in opposite ways) by an interacting cluster of regulatory molecules, including FLIP, A20, and cylindromatosis deubiquitinases, as well as the cellular regulators of apoptosis proteins, cIAP1 and cIAP2 [ 14 ]",
      "schema:name": "Hallmark of Cancer",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Unprogrammed Cell Death",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "necroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "necrosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "apoptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
