        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0008-p0",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This article section from CTARC discusses the concept of ferroptosis, a novel form of iron-dependent cell death identified by Dixon and colleagues, highlighting its relevance in cancer therapy and the role of iron as a crucial micronutrient.\n\nDixon and colleagues coined the term ferroptosis to explain the non-apoptotic, iron-dependent mechanism of cell death characterized by intracellular lipid ROS accumulation after Dolma discovered in 2003 that their test compound \"Erastin\" induced cell death in cancer cells that was distinct from all other forms of cell death [ 20 ]. For both plants and animals, iron is a vital micronutrient because it is involved in several metabolic activities, including respiration, photosynthesis, and DNA synthesis. On the other hand, an excess of iron can be harmful to the body through a few ways, including cell death [ 21 ].",
      "schema:name": "Ferroptosis",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
