    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0008-p1",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from a CTARC article discusses the harmful effects of excess iron on the body, specifically detailing ferroptosis as an iron-dependent mechanism of cell death characterized by lipid peroxidation and unique mitochondrial features.\n\nOn the other hand, an excess of iron can be harmful to the body through a few ways, including cell death [ 21 ]. Uncontrolled lipid peroxidation and the ensuing membrane damage are the causes of ferroptosis, an iron-dependent mechanism of cell death [ 97 ]. It is morphologically defined by the presence of smaller-than-normal mitochondria with condensed mitochondrial membrane densities, as well as by the rupture of the outer mitochondrial membrane and the reduction or lack of mitochondrial crista [ 97 ]. In certain normal cells as well as malignant cells, it can be induced by novel chemicals or medications (such as artesunate, sorafenib, and sulfasalazine). Renal tubule cells, neurons, fibroblasts, and T cells are a few examples (X song et al., 2016). In terms of anatomy and biochemistry, ferroptosis is an oxidative iron-dependent mechanism that is distinct from necrosis, autophagy, and apoptosis [ 77 ].",
      "schema:name": "Ferroptosis",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
