    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0008-p5",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the article discusses the morphological features of ferroptosis, highlighting the rapid cell propagation and specific ultrastructural anomalies observed in mitochondria during the process.\n\nNotably, ferroptosis that starts in one cell has the ability to rapidly propagate to neighboring cells [ 85 ]. Ferroptotic cells typically show ultrastructural anomalies related to mitochondria, including swelling or condensation, increased membrane density, absent or diminished crista, and outer membrane rupture [ 92 ]. The part played by these organelles in ferroptosis is still up for debate, despite these notable modifications in mitochondrial morphology. In most mammalian cells, mitochondria serve as both the hub of metabolism and a significant generator of reactive oxygen species (ROS) [ 8 ]. More recent research indicates that lipid peroxidation and ferroptosis induction are dependent on mitochondria-mediated ROS production, DNA damage, and metabolic reprogramming, in contrast to an earlier study that showed the induction of ferroptosis does not require mitochondria-mediated ROS generation [ 8 ].",
      "schema:name": "Ferroptosis",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Morphological features",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "mitochondria",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "reactive oxygen species",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:predicate": "relates_to",
          "beacon:object": "mitochondria",
          "beacon:evidence": "ultrastructural anomalies related to mitochondria"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "mitochondria",
          "beacon:predicate": "produces",
          "beacon:object": "reactive oxygen species",
          "beacon:evidence": "a significant generator of reactive oxygen species (ROS)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0008-p6",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This biochemical features section from Oluwafemi Adeleke Ojo's article discusses the dependence of lipid peroxidation and ferroptosis on mitochondria-mediated ROS production, contrasting previous findings and highlighting key mechanisms involved in ferroptosis.\n\nMore recent research indicates that lipid peroxidation and ferroptosis induction are dependent on mitochondria-mediated ROS production, DNA damage, and metabolic reprogramming, in contrast to an earlier study that showed the induction of ferroptosis does not require mitochondria-mediated ROS generation [ 8 ]. ### Biochemical features\n\nIron buildup and lipid peroxidation are the two primary biochemical features linked to ferroptosis, a ROS-dependent kind of cell death (Endale et al., 2023).\n\nIron accumulation: Erasin and RSL3, two traditional ferroptosis activators, enhance intracellular iron buildup while suppressing the antioxidant system [ 106 ]. Oxidative damage can be made worse by iron directly producing too many ROS through the Fenton reaction [ 104 ]. Moreover, iron may increase the activity of lipoxygenase (ALOX) and EGLN prolyl hydroxylases (often referred to as PHD), the enzymes that maintain oxygen homeostasis and cause lipid peroxidation [ 85 ]. Ferroptosis sensitivity is influenced by the interplay between cellular iron regulatory processes at the local and systemic levels. Effective inhibition of ferroptotic cell death can be achieved by targeting genes linked to iron overload or by employing iron-chelating medications (explained subsequently) [ 85 ]. The reason why iron is the only metal that may cause ferroptosis is unknown; other metals, like zinc, can also cause a Fenton reaction that produces ROS. According to one idea, iron overload causes specific downstream effectors that aid in ferroptosis to occur once lipid ROS are generated [ 85 ].",
      "schema:name": "Ferroptosis",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Biochemical features",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Oluwafemi Adeleke Ojo",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "lipid peroxidation",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
