      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0008-p7",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the article discusses the biochemical features of ferroptosis, focusing on the role of iron overload and lipid peroxidation in generating reactive byproducts that contribute to the process.\n\nAccording to one idea, iron overload causes specific downstream effectors that aid in ferroptosis to occur once lipid ROS are generated [ 85 ]. Lipid peroxidation: Lipid peroxidation, mostly affecting the cell membrane's unsaturated fatty acid content, is a free radical-induced process [ 7 ]. Lipid peroxidation produces byproducts called initially lipid hydroperoxides (LOOHs) and later reactive aldehydes, like malondialdehyde (MDA) and 4-hydroxynonenal (4HNE), which rise during ferroptosis [ 7 ]. The three distinct forms of fatty acids are saturated fatty acids (no double bond), polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFAs, >1 double bond), and monounsaturated fatty acids (MUFAs, 1 double bond) [ 23 ]. Ferroptosis appears to be primarily dependent on the peroxidation of polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFAs) in phospholipids by ALOXs, despite the possibility of oxidation of other cell membrane lipids such as cardiolipin, phosphatidylcholine, and phosphatidylethanolamine (PE) [ 64 ]. Cardiolipin peroxidation is absent from ferroptosis, even though mitochondria experience significant alterations during this process [ 85 ].",
      "schema:name": "Ferroptosis",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Biochemical features",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis",
