    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0008-p9",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the article discusses genetic features influencing ferroptosis, highlighting the potential of cells with low ACSL4 levels to undergo ferroptosis and the roles of genes related to antioxidant defense and membrane repair.\n\nBut ferroptosis may not necessarily require ACSL4, therefore in certain circumstances, cells with lower levels of ACSL4 may go through ferroptosis. The activation of genes involved in antioxidant defense (e.g., the glutathione (GSH) system, 8 coenzyme Q10 (CoQ10) system, and nuclear factor, erythroid 2-like 2 (NFE2L2, also known as NRF2) transcription pathway33) and membrane repair (e.g., the endosomal sorting complexes required for transport (ESCRT)-III pathway34) limits ferroptosis-induced membrane damage [ 54 ]. Thus, cells \"decide\" whether to survive or perish in response to ferroptotic stimuli by weighing the relative importance of anti-injury and injury responses.",
      "schema:name": "Ferroptosis",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Genetic features",
      "schema:mentions": [
