    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0008-p10",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the article discusses the immune features of ferroptosis, focusing on how ferroptotic stimuli influence cell survival decisions and the resulting impacts on leukocyte subset mortality and immunological function.\n\nThus, cells \"decide\" whether to survive or perish in response to ferroptotic stimuli by weighing the relative importance of anti-injury and injury responses. ### Immune features\n\nThere are two parts to ferroptosis's immunological effects. First, leukocyte subset mortality and subsequent loss of immunological function can result from ferroptosis [ 85 ]. For instance, ferroptosis in T cells brought on by lipid peroxidation encourages the growth of parasitic or viral infections. More significantly, and second, ferroptosis controls how the immune system responds to dying cells or their corpses when it affects non-leukocytic cells [ 70 ]. By releasing and activating distinct damage-associated molecular pattern (DAMP) signals, various forms of cellular death can trigger distinct immunological and inflammatory reactions [ 46 ].",
      "schema:name": "Ferroptosis",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Immune features",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "T cells",
          "beacon:entityType": "CELL_LINE",
