        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0014-p1",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the article discusses how acquired drug resistance in anticancer therapy is challenged by the selective ferroptosis dependence in certain cancers, particularly through the suppression of GPX4.\n\nOne of the major obstacles preventing targeted anticancer therapy from producing better results in terms of stability and integrity is acquired drug resistance (Groenendijk et al., 2014). Remarkably, it has been demonstrated that cancers of the prostate, pancreas, non-small cell lung, and melanoma cells all exhibit a selective ferroptosis dependence that is exacerbated by GPX4 loss of function [ 29 ]. Higher clinical stages and selective sensitivity to ferroptosis in cancer cells with a strong mesenchymal state indicate that these cells may be susceptible to GPX4 inhibition [ 87 ]. Ionizing radiation (IR) has been found to induce ferroptosis in esophageal adenocarcinoma, breast malignancy, renal carcinoma, and fibrosarcoma in a few investigations [ 48 ]. Furthermore, it has been discovered that, in xenograft models, ferroptosis inducers that target GPX4 increase the sensitivity of radiation therapy [ 48 ]. When comparing the doxorubicin-treated animals to the naked control mice, the tumor mass made up of gpx4-knocked HCT116 (a human colon cancer cell line) cells was somewhat less [ 83 ]. As a result of GPX4′s potency as a ferroptosis inhibitor, tumor therapies are now more drug-resistant, which poses a significant challenge in clinical settings when utilizing novel molecular targets and therapeutic modalities [ 95 ].",
      "schema:name": "Mechanisms of Ferroptosis and Cancer Therapy",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Inducing Ferroptosis by suppressing GPX4 in cancer therapy",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "prostate",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "pancreas",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "non-small cell lung",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "melanoma",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "GPX4",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "prostate",
          "beacon:predicate": "associated_with",
          "beacon:object": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:evidence": "cancers of the prostate... exhibit a selective ferroptosis dependence"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "pancreas",
          "beacon:predicate": "associated_with",
