    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0014-p3",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the article discusses the role of GPX4 in tumor development and its suppression as a strategy to induce ferroptosis in cancer therapy, highlighting the relationship between oxidative stress and cancer cell vulnerability.\n\nTherefore, it was determined that by controlling 12/15-LOX activity, GPX4 is a crucial regulator of tumor development and vascular maturation. Downregulating GPX4-induced antioxidation is a common strategy for GPX4-relevant cancer therapy because it is a well-known way to activate ferroptosis. According to studies, most cancer cells are thought to be under high levels of oxidative stress (Szatrowski et al., 1991). This suggests that to prevent oxidative damage, it is necessary to increase the ROS-scavenging ability [ 37 ]. It was discovered that ferroptosis was suppressed in cells with upregulated GPX4 expression, while it was more responsive in cells with downregulated GPX4 expression. As a ferroptosis inducer, RSL3 directly inhibits GPX4 function, which reduces antioxidant potential in cells and causes ROS to build up, ultimately resulting in ferroptosis [ 102 ]. However, DPI7 and DPI10 directly affect glutathione peroxidase, which results in ferroptosis. The amino acid selenocysteine plays a significant role in the GPX4 active group [ 89 ].",
      "schema:name": "Mechanisms of Ferroptosis and Cancer Therapy",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Inducing Ferroptosis by suppressing GPX4 in cancer therapy",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "GPX4",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "12/15-LOX",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "oxidative stress",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ROS-scavenging",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "GPX4",
          "beacon:predicate": "supports",
          "beacon:object": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:evidence": "GPX4 is a crucial regulator of tumor development and vascular maturation."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "GPX4",
          "beacon:predicate": "produces",
          "beacon:object": "oxidative stress",
          "beacon:evidence": "most cancer cells are thought to be under high levels of oxidative stress."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "GPX4",
          "beacon:predicate": "requires",
          "beacon:object": "ROS-scavenging ability",
          "beacon:evidence": "it is necessary to increase the ROS-scavenging ability."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "GPX4",
          "beacon:predicate": "causes",
          "beacon:object": "antioxidation",
          "beacon:evidence": "Downregulating GPX4-induced antioxidation is a common strategy."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "GPX4",
          "beacon:predicate": "contradicts",
          "beacon:object": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:evidence": "ferroptosis was suppressed in cells with upregulated GPX4 expression."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0014-p4",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This other section from the article discusses the role of selenocysteine in regulating GPX4 synthesis through the mevalonate pathway, highlighting its importance in inducing ferroptosis as a cancer therapy strategy.\n\nThe amino acid selenocysteine plays a significant role in the GPX4 active group [ 89 ]. To inject selenocysteine into GPX4, selenocysteine tRNA is necessary. Mevalonate (MVA) route controls selenocysteine tRNA maturation, which affects GPX4 production. Ferroptosis is therefore regulated. Two of the most crucial elements of the MVA pathway are IPP and COQ10. Reduced selenocysteine tRNA synthesis, decreased GPX4 activity, and ferroptosis are the results of inhibiting the MVA pathway [ 52 ].",
      "schema:name": "Mechanisms of Ferroptosis and Cancer Therapy",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Inducing Ferroptosis by suppressing GPX4 in cancer therapy",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "selenocysteine",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
