    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0014-p6",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the article discusses the role of the tumor suppressor gene P53 in mediating ferroptosis and its implications in cancer therapy, highlighting its significance in tumor progression despite being underexplored.\n\nThis leads to a decline in ferroptosis, lipid ROS accumulation, and cell antioxidant capacity [ 79 ]. ### P53-Mediated Ferroptosis\n\nTumor suppressor genes like P53 are crucial in this regard. P53′s involvement in ferroptosis remains unknown, even though age, apoptosis, and cell cycle suppression are all significant contributors in the onset and spread of tumors [ 56 ]. Ferroptosis is aided by P53 mutants with aberrant acetylation that have recently been discovered. Fer-1, an inhibitor of ferroptosis, caused a significant reduction in the rate of cell death, and P53 also caused ferroptosis [ 52 ]. Further research indicates that P53 can impede system Xc-uptake of cystine by downregulating SLC7A11 expression, which affects GPX4 function and reduces antioxidant capacity, ROS buildup, and ferroptosis [ 55 ].",
      "schema:name": "Mechanisms of Ferroptosis and Cancer Therapy",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "P53-Mediated Ferroptosis",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "P53",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Fer-1",
          "beacon:entityType": "TECHNOLOGY"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "P53",
          "beacon:predicate": "causes",
          "beacon:object": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:evidence": "P53 also caused ferroptosis"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "P53",
          "beacon:predicate": "supports",
          "beacon:object": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:evidence": "P53′s involvement in ferroptosis remains unknown"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "Fer-1",
          "beacon:predicate": "produces",
          "beacon:object": "a significant reduction in the rate of cell death",
          "beacon:evidence": "Fer-1, an inhibitor of ferroptosis, caused a significant reduction in the rate of cell death"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0014-p7",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the article discusses how P53 regulates ferroptosis through the downregulation of SLC7A11, affecting GPX4 function and the P53-SAT1-ALOX15 pathway, highlighting its role in cancer therapy.\n\nFurther research indicates that P53 can impede system Xc-uptake of cystine by downregulating SLC7A11 expression, which affects GPX4 function and reduces antioxidant capacity, ROS buildup, and ferroptosis [ 55 ]. Furthermore, ferroptosis regulation is mediated by the P53-SAT1-ALOX15 pathway. P53′s transcriptional target and rate-limiting enzyme for polyamine catabolism is SAT1. In response to reactive oxygen species, SAT1 activation promotes lipid peroxidation (ROS) and ferroptosis, which is connected to the production of arachidonate lipoxygenase 15. (ALOX-15) [ 94 ].",
      "schema:name": "Mechanisms of Ferroptosis and Cancer Therapy",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "P53-Mediated Ferroptosis",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
