      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0014-p9",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the article discusses how integrating ferroptosis into cancer therapy can improve immunotherapy by inducing immunogenic cell death and enhancing anti-tumor immune responses through the release of damage-associated molecular patterns.\n\n2 ### Ferroptosis and Immunotherapy\n\nThe integration of ferroptosis into existing cancer treatment modalities holds significant promise can enhance anti-tumor immune responses by inducing immunogenic cell death (ICD), releasing damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs) that activate immune cells. This process enhances the effectiveness of immunotherapy by promoting tumor antigen presentation and T cell activation. Research highlights that combining ferroptosis inducers with immune checkpoint inhibitors can overcome tumor resistance. Studies have shown that immune cells, such as CD8+ T cells, can induce ferroptosis in tumor cells, creating a synergistic anti-tumor effect [ 12 ].",
      "schema:name": "Mechanisms of Ferroptosis and Cancer Therapy",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Ferroptosis and Immunotherapy",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "immunotherapy",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "immunogenic cell death",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "damage-associated molecular patterns",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "immune checkpoint inhibitors",
