    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0014-p10",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the article discusses how ferroptosis can enhance the effectiveness of radiotherapy in cancer treatment by increasing oxidative stress and iron availability, thereby sensitizing tumor cells to ferroptotic processes.\n\nStudies have shown that immune cells, such as CD8+ T cells, can induce ferroptosis in tumor cells, creating a synergistic anti-tumor effect [ 12 ]. ### Ferroptosis and Radiotherapy\n\nWhen combined with radiotherapy, ferroptosis can create a synergistic effect. Radiotherapy induces oxidative stress and increases iron availability, both of which sensitize tumor cells to ferroptosis. Therefore, combining radiotherapy with ferroptosis inducers can amplify tumor cell death. Furthermore, the ICD induced by radiotherapy can be potentiated by concurrent ferroptosis, leading to a stronger anti-tumor immune response. This means, the radiation can start the process of cell death, and the ferroptosis can greatly increase the number of cells that are killed [ 59 ].",
      "schema:name": "Mechanisms of Ferroptosis and Cancer Therapy",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Ferroptosis and Radiotherapy",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "CD8+ T cells",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "CD8+ T cells",
          "beacon:predicate": "induces",
          "beacon:object": "ferroptosis in tumor cells",
          "beacon:evidence": "immune cells, such as CD8+ T cells, can induce ferroptosis in tumor cells"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0014-p11",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the article discusses how nanotherapy can enhance the delivery of ferroptosis inducers to tumor cells, thus minimizing off-target effects and improving cancer therapy outcomes.\n\nThis means, the radiation can start the process of cell death, and the ferroptosis can greatly increase the number of cells that are killed [ 59 ]. ### Ferroptosis and Nanotherapy\n\nNanotherapy offers a platform for targeted delivery of ferroptosis inducers to tumor cells, minimizing off-target effects. Nanoparticles can be engineered to encapsulate and release ferroptosis-inducing agents, minimizing off-target effects and maximizing therapeutic efficacy [ 88 ]. Additionally, nanoparticles can be designed to carry multiple therapeutic payloads, enabling the simultaneous delivery of ferroptosis inducers and other anti-cancer drugs, such as immunotherapy drugs, or radiosensitizers. This allows for a very targeted approach to delivering the cell death mechanism, directly to the tumor and reduces toxicity [ 1 ].",
      "schema:name": "Mechanisms of Ferroptosis and Cancer Therapy",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Ferroptosis and Nanotherapy",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "nanotherapy",
          "beacon:entityType": "METHOD"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "nanoparticles",
          "beacon:entityType": "TECHNOLOGY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis inducers",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "nanotherapy",
          "beacon:predicate": "enables",
          "beacon:object": "delivery of ferroptosis inducers to tumor cells",
          "beacon:evidence": "nanotherapy can enhance the delivery of ferroptosis inducers to tumor cells"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "nanoparticles",
          "beacon:predicate": "supports",
          "beacon:object": "targeted delivery of ferroptosis inducers",
          "beacon:evidence": "Nanotherapy offers a platform for targeted delivery of ferroptosis inducers to tumor cells"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "nanoparticles",
          "beacon:predicate": "produces",
          "beacon:object": "ferroptosis-inducing agents",
          "beacon:evidence": "Nanoparticles can be engineered to encapsulate and release ferroptosis-inducing agents"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "nanoparticles",
          "beacon:predicate": "enables",
          "beacon:object": "simultaneous delivery of ferroptosis inducers and other anti-cancer drugs",
          "beacon:evidence": "enabling the simultaneous delivery of ferroptosis inducers and other anti-cancer drugs"
        }
      ]
