          "beacon:predicate": "supports",
          "beacon:object": "glutathione synthesis",
          "beacon:evidence": "system Xc- imports cystine, a precursor for glutathione synthesis"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "iron homeostasis",
          "beacon:predicate": "requires",
          "beacon:object": "ferritin",
          "beacon:evidence": "Iron homeostasis, regulated by proteins like ferritin and transferrin"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "iron homeostasis",
          "beacon:predicate": "requires",
          "beacon:object": "transferrin",
          "beacon:evidence": "Iron homeostasis, regulated by proteins like ferritin and transferrin"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0022-p1",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This other section from the article discusses the critical interactions and context-dependent roles of ferroptosis in tumors, highlighting its potential as a therapeutic target in specific cancer types.\n\nThese interactions are critical for understanding disease progression and therapeutic targeting (Stockwell et al., 2020; [ 101 ]). In various tumors, the role of ferroptosis is complex and context-dependent. Some tumors exhibit increased sensitivity to ferroptosis, making it a promising therapeutic target. For instance, certain cancer types with GPX4 deficiency or increased iron uptake are more vulnerable. Conversely, some tumors develop resistance through upregulation of anti-ferroptotic mechanisms or alterations in lipid metabolism (Jiang et al., 2021). The interplay between ferroptosis and the tumor microenvironment, including interactions with immune cells and stromal components, further influences its impact. Notably, ferroptosis can induce immunogenic cell death, potentially enhancing anti-tumor immune responses. Recent research has shown that ferroptosis can have a role in drug resistance, and that overcoming that resistance through ferroptosis targeted therapies is a promising area of research [ 1 , 13 , 100 ].",
      "schema:name": "Ferroptosis regulation and its Role in Physiological Conditions",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "GPX4",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Stockwell et al.",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Jiang et al.",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:predicate": "supports",
          "beacon:object": "therapeutic target",
          "beacon:evidence": "its potential as a therapeutic target in specific cancer types."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "GPX4",
          "beacon:predicate": "requires",
          "beacon:object": "sensitivity to ferroptosis",
          "beacon:evidence": "certain cancer types with GPX4 deficiency or increased iron uptake are more vulnerable."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:predicate": "associated_with",
          "beacon:object": "tumor microenvironment",
          "beacon:evidence": "The interplay between ferroptosis and the tumor microenvironment."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0022-p2",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the article discusses preclinical evidence and translational studies highlighting the efficacy of inducing ferroptosis in combating drug resistance in various cancer models.\n\nRecent research has shown that ferroptosis can have a role in drug resistance, and that overcoming that resistance through ferroptosis targeted therapies is a promising area of research [ 1 , 13 , 100 ]. ### Preclinical evidence and translational studies on ferroptosis in Cancer therapy\n\nPreclinical evidence strongly supports the efficacy of ferroptosis induction in various cancer models. Studies have demonstrated that ferroptosis inducers can effectively suppress tumor growth in vitro and in vivo, particularly in cancer types resistant to conventional therapies. For example, research has shown that GPX4 inhibitors can induce ferroptosis and inhibit tumor progression in several cancer types, including hepatocellular carcinoma and pancreatic cancer. Additionally, combination therapies, such as ferroptosis inducers with immune checkpoint inhibitors or radiotherapy, have shown synergistic effects in preclinical settings (Tang et al., 2021; Stockwell et al., 2020).",
      "schema:name": "Ferroptosis regulation and its Role in Physiological Conditions",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Preclinical evidence and translational studies on ferroptosis in Cancer therapy",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
