        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "4-hydroxy-2-nonenal",
          "beacon:predicate": "produces",
          "beacon:object": "lipid peroxidation",
          "beacon:evidence": "This can be measured using specific assays like the detection of 4-hydroxy-2-nonenal (4-HNE)"
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:predicate": "associated_with",
          "beacon:object": "reduced glutathione levels",
          "beacon:evidence": "Ferroptotic cells often exhibit decreased levels of glutathione (GSH)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0024-p2",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the Morphological and biochemical indications of Ferroptosis section discusses the biochemical indicators of ferroptosis, specifically reduced glutathione levels and increased iron levels, which are critical in understanding oxidative stress and lipid peroxidation.\n\nReduced Glutathione Levels : Ferroptotic cells often exhibit decreased levels of glutathione (GSH), a crucial antioxidant that helps protect against oxidative stress [ 40 ]. Increased Iron Levels: Ferroptosis is associated with elevated intracellular iron levels, which catalyze the formation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and contribute to lipid peroxidation (Endale et al., 2023). Impaired Antioxidant Systems: The ferroptotic process involves the dysfunction of antioxidant systems, particularly the glutathione peroxidase 4 (GPX4) enzyme, which normally reduces lipid hydroperoxides. Inhibition or depletion of GPX4 is a key trigger of ferroptosis [ 93 ]. Altered Metabolism: Changes in metabolic pathways related to iron metabolism and lipid synthesis can be indicative of ferroptosis. For example, inhibition of cysteine uptake and disruption of the cystine-glutamate antiporter (system Xc⁻) can contribute to ferroptosis [ 80 ].",
      "schema:name": "Morphological and biochemical indications of Ferroptosis",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Biochemical Indications",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Ferroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Reduced Glutathione",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
