    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0028-p0",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This section from the article discusses recent advancements in drug delivery systems aimed at enhancing ferroptosis-inducing agents' efficacy and minimizing off-target effects, highlighting the potential of nanoparticles, liposomes, and micelles in targeting tumor cells.\n\nRecent advancements in drug delivery systems have focused on enhancing the efficacy of ferroptosis-inducing agents while minimizing off-target effects. Novel drug delivery systems, such as nanoparticles, liposomes, and micelles, have shown promise in targeting ferroptosis in tumor cells. For instance, iron oxide nanoparticles can facilitate the localized release of ferroptosis inducers, thereby enhancing their cytotoxic effects in malignant tissues while sparing normal cells [ 36 ]. These systems can be engineered to respond to specific tumor microenvironmental cues, such as pH or enzymatic activity, ensuring that ferroptosis is selectively induced in cancer cells.",
      "schema:name": "Novel Drug Delivery Systems Targeting Ferroptosis in Cancer Therapy and Clinical Implications",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "nanoparticles",
          "beacon:entityType": "TECHNOLOGY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "liposomes",
          "beacon:entityType": "TECHNOLOGY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "micelles",
          "beacon:entityType": "TECHNOLOGY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "iron oxide nanoparticles",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "iron oxide nanoparticles",
          "beacon:predicate": "supports",
          "beacon:object": "localized release of ferroptosis inducers",
          "beacon:evidence": "iron oxide nanoparticles can facilitate the localized release of ferroptosis inducers"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0028-p1",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This novel drug delivery systems section from the article discusses engineered systems designed to selectively induce ferroptosis in cancer cells by responding to tumor microenvironmental cues and highlights specific ferroptosis-inducing drugs such as RSL3 and erastin.\n\nThese systems can be engineered to respond to specific tumor microenvironmental cues, such as pH or enzymatic activity, ensuring that ferroptosis is selectively induced in cancer cells. Several novel ferroptosis-inducing drugs have been developed, including RSL3 and erastin, which inhibit the system Xc- cystine/glutamate antiporter, leading to glutathione depletion and subsequent lipid peroxidation [ 98 ]. These agents have shown efficacy in various cancer models, particularly in those resistant to conventional therapies. The combination of ferroptosis inducers with traditional chemotherapeutics is an area of active research, as it may enhance therapeutic outcomes. For example, combining ferroptosis inducers with doxorubicin has demonstrated synergistic effects in breast cancer models, suggesting that ferroptosis can be exploited to overcome drug resistance [ 16 ].",
      "schema:name": "Novel Drug Delivery Systems Targeting Ferroptosis in Cancer Therapy and Clinical Implications",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "RSL3",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
