        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0028-p3",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the article discusses the innovative manipulation of ferroptosis as a promising strategy in cancer therapy aimed at effectively targeting and eliminating refractory tumors.\n\nThis combination approach could revolutionize cancer treatment paradigms, offering new avenues for patients with refractory tumors. ### Targeting Ferroptosis in Cancer Therapy\n\nThe manipulation of ferroptosis presents an innovative approach to cancer therapy. Several studies have demonstrated that inducing ferroptosis can effectively kill cancer cells, particularly those resistant to conventional therapies. For instance, cancer cells with mutations in the p53 tumor suppressor gene exhibit increased sensitivity to ferroptosis inducers, suggesting that ferroptosis can be exploited in p53-deficient tumors [ 67 ]. Additionally, certain chemotherapeutic agents, such as sorafenib and erastin, have been shown to induce ferroptosis in various cancer types, including liver and pancreatic cancers [ 96 , 98 ]. By integrating ferroptosis inducers into existing treatment regimens, clinicians may enhance the efficacy of current therapies, particularly in hard-to-treat cancers.",
      "schema:name": "Novel Drug Delivery Systems Targeting Ferroptosis in Cancer Therapy and Clinical Implications",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Targeting Ferroptosis in Cancer Therapy",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "p53",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "p53",
          "beacon:predicate": "associated_with",
          "beacon:object": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:evidence": "suggesting that ferroptosis can be exploited in p53-deficient tumors"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0028-p4",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This novel drug delivery systems section from the article discusses integrating ferroptosis inducers into treatments to combat drug resistance in cancer therapy.\n\nBy integrating ferroptosis inducers into existing treatment regimens, clinicians may enhance the efficacy of current therapies, particularly in hard-to-treat cancers. ### Overcoming Drug Resistance\n\nOne of the most significant challenges in cancer treatment is drug resistance. Ferroptosis offers a promising avenue for overcoming this obstacle. For instance, cancer cells that develop resistance to traditional therapies often maintain a degree of sensitivity to ferroptosis. This has been observed in models of drug-resistant breast cancer, where ferroptosis induction restored sensitivity to chemotherapeutic agents [ 25 ]. The ability to combine ferroptosis inducers with existing therapies could provide a dual mechanism of action, attacking cancer cells through multiple pathways and reducing the likelihood of resistance developing.",
      "schema:name": "Novel Drug Delivery Systems Targeting Ferroptosis in Cancer Therapy and Clinical Implications",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Overcoming Drug Resistance",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "drug-resistant breast cancer",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "chemotherapeutic agents",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:predicate": "supports",
          "beacon:object": "drug-resistant breast cancer",
          "beacon:evidence": "ferroptosis induction restored sensitivity to chemotherapeutic agents"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0028-p5",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the article discusses the importance of identifying biomarkers for ferroptosis to predict sensitivity and enhance the clinical application of dual-action cancer therapies.\n\nThe ability to combine ferroptosis inducers with existing therapies could provide a dual mechanism of action, attacking cancer cells through multiple pathways and reducing the likelihood of resistance developing. ### Biomarkers for Ferroptosis\n\nIdentifying biomarkers that predict sensitivity to ferroptosis is crucial for the clinical application of this therapeutic strategy. Current research is focused on understanding the genetic and metabolic profiles that correlate with ferroptosis susceptibility. For example, alterations in the expression of genes involved in iron metabolism, such as SLC7A11, have been linked to ferroptosis sensitivity [ 20 ]. The development of reliable biomarkers could facilitate patient stratification, allowing clinicians to identify those who would benefit most from ferroptosis-targeting therapies. This personalized approach could enhance treatment efficacy and minimize unnecessary toxicity in patients unlikely to respond.",
      "schema:name": "Novel Drug Delivery Systems Targeting Ferroptosis in Cancer Therapy and Clinical Implications",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Biomarkers for Ferroptosis",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "SLC7A11",
          "beacon:entityType": "ENTITY"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "SLC7A11",
          "beacon:predicate": "associated_with",
          "beacon:object": "ferroptosis sensitivity",
          "beacon:evidence": "alterations in the expression of genes involved in iron metabolism, such as SLC7A11, have been linked to ferroptosis sensitivity"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0028-p6",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the article discusses safety and toxicity considerations in the context of ferroptosis induction, highlighting concerns about the accumulation of lipid peroxides and their potential adverse effects on normal tissues.\n\nThis personalized approach could enhance treatment efficacy and minimize unnecessary toxicity in patients unlikely to respond. ### Safety and Toxicity Considerations\n\nWhile the induction of ferroptosis holds promise, it is essential to consider the potential safety and toxicity implications. The accumulation of lipid peroxides can have deleterious effects on normal tissues, raising concerns about the therapeutic window of ferroptosis inducers. Preclinical studies have indicated that the systemic induction of ferroptosis can lead to organ damage, particularly in the liver and kidneys [ 103 ]. Thus, careful monitoring and management of potential side effects will be necessary in clinical settings. The development of targeted delivery systems that confine ferroptosis inducers to tumor tissues may mitigate these risks, enhancing safety while maximizing therapeutic efficacy.",
      "schema:name": "Novel Drug Delivery Systems Targeting Ferroptosis in Cancer Therapy and Clinical Implications",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Safety and Toxicity Considerations",
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "lipid peroxides",
          "beacon:predicate": "produces",
          "beacon:object": "deleterious effects on normal tissues",
          "beacon:evidence": "The accumulation of lipid peroxides can have deleterious effects on normal tissues."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:predicate": "supports",
          "beacon:object": "treatment efficacy",
          "beacon:evidence": "This personalized approach could enhance treatment efficacy."
        },
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "ferroptosis inducers",
          "beacon:predicate": "requires",
          "beacon:object": "careful monitoring and management of potential side effects",
          "beacon:evidence": "Thus, careful monitoring and management of potential side effe."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0028-p7",
      "beacon:sectionType": "other",
      "schema:text": "This subsection from the article discusses the challenges and future directions in the development of targeted drug delivery systems for ferroptosis in cancer therapy, highlighting the need to address the regulation complexity and tumor heterogeneity.\n\nThe development of targeted delivery systems that confine ferroptosis inducers to tumor tissues may mitigate these risks, enhancing safety while maximizing therapeutic efficacy. ### Challenges and Future Directions\n\nDespite the promising potential of ferroptosis in cancer therapy, several challenges remain. The complexity of ferroptosis regulation, coupled with the heterogeneity of tumors, necessitates further research to elucidate the precise mechanisms governing ferroptosis in different cancer types. Moreover, the translation of preclinical findings into clinical practice requires robust clinical trials to establish the safety, efficacy, and optimal dosing regimens of ferroptosis inducers. Future studies should also explore combination strategies that integrate ferroptosis with other therapeutic modalities, such as immunotherapy and targeted therapies, to enhance overall treatment outcomes.",
      "schema:name": "Novel Drug Delivery Systems Targeting Ferroptosis in Cancer Therapy and Clinical Implications",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Challenges and Future Directions"
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-ctarc-2025-100913-sec0034-p0",
      "beacon:sectionType": "conclusion",
      "schema:text": "This conclusion section from the article discusses the potential of ferroptosis in revolutionizing cancer therapy by addressing drug resistance and enhancing treatment efficacy through understanding its mechanisms and identifying relevant biomarkers.\n\nFerroptosis represents a novel and promising approach to cancer therapy, with the potential to overcome drug resistance and improve treatment efficacy. By understanding the mechanisms underlying ferroptosis and identifying biomarkers for sensitivity, clinicians may harness this form of regulated cell death to develop more effective cancer therapies. The integration of developing biological discoveries and technological innovations with conventional therapy is necessary in the therapy of cancer. Ferroptosis a recently discovered form of cell death that is dependent on lipid peroxidation and is distinct from classical programmed cell death. Many studies have demonstrated that ferroptosis plays a critical function in destroying tumor cells and reducing tumor growth, which is something that current cancer treatments cannot properly handle given the resilience of cancer cells to existing chemotherapeutic medications.",
      "schema:name": "Conclusion",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "ferroptosis",
          "beacon:entityType": "CONCEPT"
        },
        {
